+        "EE-070",
+        "EE-082"
+      ],
+      "decision": "Setup asks where each of the venture's functions keeps its truth, and records the answers as pointers in knowledge/sources.json under `functions` (the source-of-truth matrix, EE-042, not a second file), with `truth` naming where the venture's source of truth as a whole lives (local by default). schema/systems-catalog.json lists the systems offered per function (a local repo, GitHub, Vercel, Cloudflare, Stripe, Square, Shopify, Rho, Google Drive, DocuSign, a local folder). A system Naive reads through code is connected (Rho, through its MCP API: sandbox, live with RHO_API_TOKEN, or a saved fixture marked as one); the rest are pointed until a connector exists. A function with no system keeps the founder's words. naive dev serves a setup page at /setup (interface/dev/setup.mjs) that walks through people and hats, sources of truth, the strategic foundation and first decisions (each answer an approved decision; a foundation statement as a decision with `statement`), and a goal for discovery, and says when the structure is established and what comes next. A new venture's .gitignore keeps sensitive stream activity out of git.",
+      "why": "Torian, 2026-10-29: when scaffolding, ask the user to point Naive to the source of truth for each function (the repo, GitHub and Vercel for a software product; analytics from the host; Stripe, Square or Shopify for sales; Rho for financials in the demo; legal documents, with the connections to set them up if missing), let a function default or be answered in prose, and ship a local setup experience like the one Next.js gives, essentially the strategic foundation and a version of the decision docket, ending by setting a goal for the discovery phase. After setup a founder has the structure of the venture and a way of routing decisions; operating it is Mentura's, as deploying is Vercel's after Next.js."
     }
