@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+type: decision-note
+status: draft
+confidential: true
+source: hybrid
+created: 2026-10-29T08:00:00Z
+ai-model: claude-opus-5.5
+tags: [mentura, platform, frames, viability, research, forum, library-api, confidential]
+description: "Architecture decision note: a frame's verdict separates possible from viable (possible, available, acceptable, confirmed). The simple version is built; real-world research with investors and a gated library API are recorded as options to consider."
+---
+
+# Viability and real-world research
+
+**Status:** the simple version is decided and built (Torian, 2026-10-29: "I will take your recommendations"). Everything under "To consider" is an option, not a decision.
+
+**Confidential:** Mentura only.
+
+## Problem
+
+Running the "fund without ceding control" frame on a constructed founder returned "false dichotomy": outside money does not have to mean selling shares. Torian's objection, in his words (2026-10-29):
+
+> we cannot be sure that there are any investors who would be willing to invest at this phase for the kind of shares or the kind of stake or the specific type of deal that the founder is looking for. So even though the statement is true that Jev gave, it may not be a viable solution for once everything is considered.
+
+Possible in principle is not the same as viable for this founder now.
+
+## Decision (built)
+
+Each option a frame's lookup finds climbs a ladder. A stage counts only when every stage before it holds.
+
+| Stage | Question | Who answers | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| possible | Is the trade false, or a sequence? | Naive (`schema/frames.mjs`, Jev on the frame's premises) | the venture's facts |
+| available | Do the source's own eligibility criteria include this venture today? | Mentura's runner, Jev per option | what the source says (desk research) |
+| acceptable | Does what the provider gets leave the founder's named control rights untouched? | Mentura's runner, Jev per option | what the source says |
+| confirmed | Has a real provider said yes? | a person: the founder, or Mentura's research service | a conversation or a forum reply |
+
+"Available" and "acceptable" are probabilities from desk research. No model can reach "confirmed". The lookup decision's note carries the ladder ("available on desk research for 1 of 4; acceptable for 1; confirmed for 0"), so the founder sees how far each option has actually been tested.
+
+Placement (recommended by Claude, accepted by Torian): the frame (Naive) holds the questions; the agents, lookups and judging run in Mentura's kit (`platform/agents/frames/run.mjs`). Naive's frames stay offline and answer-free.
+
+## To consider (not decided)
+
+1. **Real-world research is the only route to "confirmed", and also the honest route to "available".** Torian: desk research cannot see whether money is actually moving. Investors may be willing while markets are not ("the financial markets are over leveraged"). "The way we would know would be by actually talking to investors." That costs the founder time and energy they may not have. A Mentura service could do it for them: agents that talk to founders and investors, or a platform where the conversations take place.
+2. **The forum as a research source.** Mentura's anonymous founder-investor forum (Torian's idea, to be discussed separately): founders post anonymous frames with what they need, investors star or engage, and people share only their roles. Replies become evidence for "available" and "confirmed".
+3. **Answer now, firm up later.** Replies take time. The runner still gives its desk-research probability at once and updates the stage when evidence arrives, the way the docket re-renders when a decision changes.
+4. **The library as an API.** Frames could be served by an API instead of shipped. Calls could be gated so that only a project with Naive set up can use the library. This bears on the open question of what belongs in Naive and what belongs in Mentura (kits, the agent harness, or the library itself).
+5. **Is this over-engineered?** Torian asked this himself. The simple ladder may be enough until real founders use it. Revisit after the demo, with traces.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Each stage can be wrong on its own, so the training loop gets a sharper signal (see `agent-training-pipeline.md`).
+- The scout must now report each option's eligibility and what the provider gets, in the source's words.
