index 0000000..b3a34e6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/platform/design/agent-training-pipeline.md
@@ -0,0 +1,98 @@
+---
+type: design
+status: draft
+confidential: true
+source: hybrid
+created: 2026-10-29T06:57:15Z
+ai-model: claude-opus-5.5
+tags: [mentura, platform, harness, agents, langsmith, jev, decision-library, confidential]
+description: "Mentura's agent training pipeline: agents attack the premises in a decision frame, Jev judges, code decides, and LangSmith turns every run into training data. Confidential, platform only."
+---
+
+# Agent training pipeline
+
+**Status:** draft from the 2026-10-29 design discussion (thread "Design Naive's decision library"). Torian agreed with the direction and asked for it to be written down. The details below are Claude's proposal and have not been decided.
+
+**Confidential:** this belongs to Mentura, not to Naive. It is the start of Mentura's own harness (see [the home field idea](../strategy/ideas/own-harness-home-field-2026-10-29.md)).
+
+## What Naive gives it
+
+Naive's decision library holds frames: each one is the shape of a recurring venture problem, with its goal, hidden decisions, premises and the premise attacks to run. A frame never contains the answer. A frame declares what current information it needs (its freshness spec). Rendering a frame writes decision records with open options. All of that is open and works offline.
+
+Mentura runs frames. That is this pipeline.
+
+## The split
+
+| Part | Does | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| Code | frame schema, writing records, docket ranking, gates | must always behave the same way |
+| Jev (System 1) | Choice, Noul (yes/no, thresholds 0.7 and 0.3), Score | fast typed judgments on one sharp question |
+| Our agents (System 2) | turn a founder's problem into sharp questions Jev can judge | this is the skill we train |
+
+The core skill is asking Jev good questions. Jev's verdicts are only as good as the propositions it is given.
+
+## Agents
+
+1. **Framer.** Picks the frame with a Choice, then extracts the goal, the hidden decisions and the premises from the founder's words.
+2. **Premise attacker.** Runs each attack (false dichotomy, split the dimension, test the goal, sequence instead of choose) and writes one Noul proposition per premise.
+3. **Freshness scout.** Runs the frame's freshness spec at use time (Firecrawl), with dates and sources, and caches each result with an expiry.
+4. **Critic.** Checks that no answer leaked into a frame, that options came only from the scout, and that the dichotomy verdict (false, real trade-off, or sequence) holds.
+5. **Frame author.** Notices problems that recur across ventures and drafts new frames for the library. A person approves each one.
+
+## The loop in LangSmith
+
+LangSmith does not train models. It is the loop that produces the training data. Most of it already runs on the `naive-hackathon` project (`platform/integrations/langsmith-setup.mjs`):
+
+- every run is traced;
+- a run that misses a gate, or that Jev scores below 0.7, goes to the founder review queue;
+- a run that holds every gate goes to the good-runs dataset.
+
+To add for frames:
+
+- trace each agent step as its own span: frame chosen, propositions written, Jev verdicts, options found, records written;
+- a frames dataset of founder problem, propositions, Jev verdicts and the founder's call;
+- evaluators: code checks (no answer in a frame, every option cited) and Jev judges (did the reframe keep the goal, were the hidden decisions found);
+- experiments that compare prompts and models for each agent against that dataset.
+
+## Training signal
+
+1. **Now: the founder's approvals** in the review queue. They are available today. This is the proposal Torian liked on 2026-10-29.
+2. **Later: outcomes.** Decision records carry projected and actual outcomes (EE-058). Once enough have resolved, outcomes become the ground truth, and approvals become a weaker signal.
+
+## Case sources
+
+Torian ruled out his own ventures as seed cases (2026-10-29). His idea for where they come from, in his words (2026-10-29):
+
+> we actually could use episodes of Shark Tank, as well as podcasts such as David Senra and other materials to recreate the frames of different decisions and deals that have been made over time. I don't know the legal implications of this, but it's one way of gaining training data. It is also the purpose for why we will be doing our own podcasts and interviews.
+
+Why it works: a public deal has a known outcome. A reconstructed case's expected verdicts come from what actually happened, so it gives the outcome signal (EE-058) years early, without waiting for Mentura's own founders' decisions to resolve.
+
+Built (`platform/agents/cases/reconstruct.mjs`): a web agent reconstructs a deal into a case for one frame. It records the facts as they stood when the decision was made, what happened, the expected verdicts with a reason for each, and the public pages it used. Code drops any case without a source, with a label outside the frame's, or with a source note over 300 characters. Cases are `reconstructed` and `draft` until a person reviews them, and they are written outside git (`.cases/`). `agents/frames/run.mjs --case-file` scores the agents against them.
+
+Sources, cleanest first:
+
+1. **Mentura's own podcasts and interviews**, with a signed release that covers training. No question about rights. This is the point of the interview programme.
+2. **Public reporting about a deal**: show recaps, press, company statements, podcast episode pages. Deal terms and dates are facts. The code keeps only links and short paraphrases.
+3. **Transcripts, episodes and audio themselves** (Shark Tank, Founders and others): not used. Copying them, or training on them, is a copyright and terms-of-service question, and the case law on training is unsettled. Ask counsel before using any of it.
+
+Hindsight labels are an opinion too. The first live run labelled Scrub Daddy "false dichotomy", even though the founder sold 20% of the company. A person has to check every label before a case goes into the dataset.
+
+This is not legal advice. Before reconstructed cases leave Mentura or are used to train a model, get counsel to review the sources and what is stored.
+
+## Our own models
+
+Start with prompts on rented models. Fine-tune only once a dataset is large enough, and only for narrow jobs:
+
+- writing Noul propositions from premises (first candidate);
+- picking the frame (a Choice may already be enough);
+- the critic's checks.
+
+A small model trained to speak Jev's language, plus Jev, plus code, is the harness.
+
+## Open
+
+- Which signal do we trust when approvals and outcomes disagree?
+- How much data is enough before the first fine-tune?
+- Where frames live and the package and kit split are being settled on PR #33 (hackathon thread).
+- Counsel review of reconstructed cases and of training on public shows and podcasts.
+- Mentura moves to its own private repository after the hackathon demo, taking `platform/` and this file with it (Torian, 2026-10-29).
