+---
+type: idea
+status: draft
+confidential: true
+source: human
+created: 2026-10-29T06:56:38Z
+ai-model: claude-opus-5.5
+tags: [mentura, strategy, harness, confidential]
+description: "Idea draft, not a formal record: why Mentura builds its own harness. Model and harness do best when built together, so Claude models work best in Claude's own harnesses."
+---
+
+# Build our own harness: home field advantage
+
+**Status:** idea draft. This is a piece of strategy reasoning, not a decision or a formal record. It can be turned into one later.
+
+**Confidential:** internal to Mentura. It is not part of Naive and must not be published.
+
+## In Torian's words (2026-10-29, verbatim)
+
+> I would also note that the Claude models work best with Claude harnesses, and I believe that is because the Claude Code and the other Claude harnesses have home field advantage. They are able to optimize their harnesses best in relationship to the model. Even though the Claude models are great and can be used in other places, even inside of Cursor or other harnesses, the reality is they're just better inside of Claude Code. I think this is why it's important to build your own harness.
+
+## Drafted by Claude, for Torian to accept or change
+
+- A model and the harness built for it improve together. The team that owns both tunes the tools, the context and the prompts to the model, and tunes the model to that harness.
+- For Mentura, the "model" is a set of pieces: Jev (System 1), our agents, and, later, our own fine-tuned models. If we own the harness around them, we can tune it to them the same way.
+- The agent training pipeline in [`../../design/agent-training-pipeline.md`](../../design/agent-training-pipeline.md) is where that harness starts.
+- Open question: this argues for owning the harness, not for owning every model. Which pieces do we train, and which do we rent?
