+  "ok": true,
+  "result": { "ada": { "id": "ada", "name": "Ada" } },
+  "errors": { "cy": { "file": "customers/cy.json", "error": "customers/cy.json does not parse: Expected double-quoted property name in JSON at position 14 (line 1 column 15)" } }
+}
+```
+
+The answer also carries each record's metadata, left out here. Reading `customers/cy` on its own answers that record's error with the schema and template to fix it, instead of a stack trace.
+
+## A failing package
+
+This translates `error.js`: errors bubble to the nearest boundary. Each package is a boundary. A failing record or check in one package is contained there, and the other packages proceed. `mentura <package> check` checks one package; `mentura venture check` checks the venture level first (venture.json and the folder structure), then every package it uses:
+
+```sh
+node interface/cli/mentura.mjs venture check --venture wr-errors # exits 1
+rm ~/ventures/wr-errors/customers/cy.json
+node interface/cli/mentura.mjs foundation check --venture wr-errors
+```
+
+```json
+{
+  "ok": false,
+  "reached": "package",
+  "venture": { "ok": true, "warnings": [] },
+  "contained": ["customers"],
+  "proceeds": ["foundation", "validation", "product"]
+}
+```
+
+The answer is trimmed here: it also lists each package. `reached` is `package` when the failure stayed in packages and `venture` when the venture level failed. Each package lists its records with `ok`, the error and the fix, and the checks it ran. `node interface/boundaries.mjs check <venture dir>` prints the same as text.
+
+## A broken venture.json
+
+This translates `global-error.js`, which replaces the root layout when it fails. A venture.json that does not parse, lacks a required field, or has an unknown phase answers every route with what is wrong and how to recover:
+
+```sh
+printf '{ "id": "wr-errors", "name": ' > ~/ventures/wr-errors/venture.json
+node interface/cli/mentura.mjs venture read --venture wr-errors # exits 1
+node interface/boundaries.mjs recover ~/ventures/wr-errors --confirm recover
+node interface/cli/mentura.mjs venture read --venture wr-errors
+```
+
+```text
+wr-errors: venture.json does not parse: Unexpected end of JSON input (at the end of the file, line 1)
+Recovery:
+  1. Fix it by hand: it must be a JSON object with id, name, parent, phase, owner, status, layers
+  2. Or rebuild a minimal one, keeping the broken file in _private/: node interface/boundaries.mjs recover ~/ventures/wr-errors --confirm recover
+  3. Then check it: python3 schema/validate.py --schema venture.schema.json --file ~/ventures/wr-errors/venture.json
+```
+
+When the venture is in git, the first step is to restore the committed file. `recover` moves the broken file to `_private/` and writes a minimal venture.json; copy back what you need from the kept file. One broken venture never stops the router finding the others: `mentura ventures` lists it as broken.
+
+A broken framework file, such as `schema/data-model.json`, stops every route the same way, before any module reads it. The answer names the file and says to restore it and run `node schema/check-data-model.mjs`.
+
